Design a globally distributed, multilingual typeahead/autocomplete system for a job portal like Monster.com

Scenario:
When a user searches for a job role such as “Software Engineer”, the system should provide relevant job-role suggestions in real time as the user types.
Since the platform operates across multiple countries, users may search in different languages. For example, the same job role may have different names or translations 
in different countries.
The system should support high request volume, multilingual search, low latency, high availability, and a responsive user experience, with suggestions returned 
within a few milliseconds wherever possible.

Example:

A user in the US types: soft

Suggestions:
Software Engineer
Software Developer
Software Architect

A user in Germany may type the equivalent local term and expect:
Softwareentwickler
Software Engineer
Softwarearchitekt

The system should understand the language/locale and user context and return appropriate suggestions.


========= Solutions:

How I would design it
As a senior Java/backend engineer, I would not call the database for every keystroke.

The key architecture would be:
|---------------------------------------------------------|
                    Global Users
                         |
                    CDN / Edge
                         |
                Global Load Balancer
                         |
              +----------+----------+
              |                     |
          US Region             EU Region
              |                     |
       Typeahead Service     Typeahead Service
              |                     |
           Redis                 Redis
              |                     |
       Search Index          Search Index
       Elasticsearch         Elasticsearch
              |
        Job Role Dataset
|---------------------------------------------------------|

Request flow:

User types:
|---------------------------|
s
↓
so
↓
sof
↓
soft
|---------------------------|

Each keystroke can generate a request.

So instead of:

soft → Database

we want:
|---------------------------|
soft
 ↓
Redis
 ↓
[Software Engineer,
 Software Developer,
 Software Architect]
|---------------------------|
This makes the response extremely fast.

1. Debouncing on the frontend

This is the first optimization.

Don't send a request for every keystroke.

Instead:
|-----------------------------------|
User types:

s → no API
so → no API
sof → no API
soft → wait 100–200 ms
          ↓
       API call
|-----------------------------------|
For example:

debounce(search, 150ms)

This can dramatically reduce unnecessary requests.

2. Minimum characters

Don't search when the user types only:

s

or:

so

You might start searching after 2 or 3 characters.
|-----------------------------------|
s       → no request
so      → maybe no request
sof     → search
soft    → search
|-----------------------------------|

This reduces load significantly.

3. Redis for hot suggestions

This is one of the most important parts.

For popular prefixes:
|-----------------------------------|
soft
softw
softwa
|-----------------------------------|
store precomputed results.

Example:
|-----------------------------------|
Key:
typeahead:en:soft

Value:
[
  "Software Engineer",
  "Software Developer",
  "Software Architect",
  "Software Engineering Manager"
]
|-----------------------------------|

Then:
|-----------------------------------|
Request
   ↓
Redis
   ↓
Response
|-----------------------------------|
Redis can respond in sub-millisecond to low-millisecond range in appropriate deployments.

4. Don't store every possible string manually

You could have millions of job titles.

Instead, maintain a search index.

For example:

Elasticsearch/OpenSearch
|-----------------------------------|
Job Title
---------
Software Engineer
Software Developer
Senior Software Engineer
Software Engineering Manager
Java Developer
Backend Engineer
|-----------------------------------|

The search index handles:

Prefix matching
Fuzzy matching
Ranking
Tokenization
Language analyzers
Synonyms

5. Multilingual architecture

This is where your problem becomes more interesting.

I wouldn't simply translate every request dynamically.

For example:
|-----------------------------------|
English:

Software Engineer
|-----------------------------------|

could have localized equivalents:
|-----------------------------------|
English → Software Engineer
German  → Softwareentwickler
French  → Ingénieur logiciel
Spanish → Ingeniero de software
|-----------------------------------|

I'd maintain a canonical job-role ID.
|-----------------------------------|
role_id = 12345

English:
Software Engineer

German:
Softwareentwickler

French:
Ingénieur logiciel

Spanish:
Ingeniero de software
|-----------------------------------|

Now all translations refer to the same canonical role.

6. Locale-aware search

The request should contain locale information:
|------------------------------------------------------|
GET /typeahead?q=soft&locale=en-US
|------------------------------------------------------|
or:

|------------------------------------------------------|
GET /typeahead?q=software&locale=de-DE
|------------------------------------------------------|

The service can select the appropriate index/analyzer:

|------------------------------------------------------|
en-US → English index
de-DE → German index
fr-FR → French index
es-ES → Spanish index
|------------------------------------------------------|

Potential architecture:
|------------------------------------------------------|
                 Typeahead Service
                        |
                   Detect Locale
                        |
        +---------------+---------------+
        |               |               |
      English         German          French
       Index           Index            Index
|------------------------------------------------------|
For very large scale, these indexes can be distributed geographically.

7. Precompute popular prefixes

This is where you can make the system really fast.

Suppose your data contains:
|------------------------------------------------------|
Software Engineer
Software Developer
Software Architect
|------------------------------------------------------|

During an offline processing job, generate:
|------------------------------------------------------|
s
so
sof
soft
softw
softwa
software
software e
...
|------------------------------------------------------|
and associate them with ranked suggestions.

Then:
|-----------------------------|
soft
 ↓
Redis
 ↓
Top 10 results
|-----------------------------|

No expensive search operation is required for popular prefixes.

This is essentially a precomputed typeahead index.

8. Ranking

You don't just want matching results.

You want the best results.

For example:
|-----------------------------|
User types:

java
|-----------------------------|
Instead of alphabetically:
|-----------------------------|
Java Architect
Java Developer
Java Engineer
Java Programmer
|-----------------------------|
you could rank based on:
|-----------------------------|
Popularity
+
Country
+
Language
+
Historical searches
+
User context
+
Recent trends
|-----------------------------|

Result:
|-----------------------------|
1. Java Developer
2. Java Software Engineer
3. Senior Java Developer
4. Java Architect
|-----------------------------|

9. CDN / Edge caching

Because Monster-like traffic is global, I'd also consider edge caching for extremely popular/static suggestions.
|-----------------------------|
India user
     ↓
India Edge
     ↓
Typeahead service

US user
     ↓
US Edge
     ↓
Typeahead service
|-----------------------------|
This reduces network latency.

10. Multi-region deployment

For a global application:
|----------------------------------------------------|
             Global DNS
                  |
       +----------+----------+
       |          |          |
      US         EU         APAC
       |          |          |
     Redis      Redis      Redis
       |          |          |
     Search     Search     Search
|----------------------------------------------------|
Users should ideally be routed to the nearest region.

This reduces:  Network latency

and improves the "snappy" experience.

11. What happens when a new job role is added?

Don't manually update Redis.

Use an event-driven pipeline:
|----------------------------------------------------|
Admin / Job System
       |
       v
    Kafka
       |
       v
Indexing Service
       |
       +----> Elasticsearch
       |
       +----> Redis
|----------------------------------------------------|
For example:

"Generative AI Engineer"

is added.

Event:

JOB_ROLE_CREATED

Kafka publishes the event.

Indexing service updates:
|----------------------------------------------------|
Search Index
Redis
|----------------------------------------------------|

Final architecture

I'd present it as like this:
|----------------------------------------------------|
                         GLOBAL USERS
                              |
                       CDN / Edge Cache
                              |
                     Global Load Balancer
                              |
                  +-----------+-----------+
                  |                       |
              Region A                 Region B
                  |                       |
          Typeahead Service       Typeahead Service
                  |                       |
              Redis Cache              Redis Cache
                  |                       |
          Search Index             Search Index
       (OpenSearch/ES)          (OpenSearch/ES)
                  ^
                  |
             Kafka Events
                  ^
                  |
          Job Role / Master Data
|----------------------------------------------------|

Request path
|----------------------------------------------------|
User types "soft"
       ↓
Frontend debounce 150ms
       ↓
Typeahead API
       ↓
Redis
       |
       +---- HIT → return suggestions
       |
       +---- MISS
              ↓
        Search Index
              ↓
        Return + cache
|----------------------------------------------------|

Data update path
|----------------------------------------------------|
Job Role Master
      ↓
Kafka
      ↓
Indexing Service
      ↓
Search Index
      ↓
Redis
|----------------------------------------------------|



In Short:
For a globally distributed typeahead system, I would keep the synchronous request path extremely lightweight: debounce on the client, route to the nearest region, serve hot prefixes 
from Redis, and fall back to a locale-aware search index. The expensive indexing, translation and ranking work should happen asynchronously.
